  <h2 id="saear">Training your ears with a weekly routine</h2>

  <p>
    Good audio work depends on hearing small differences. A short weekly routine builds this skill faster than occasional long sessions.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Day</th>
          <th>Exercise</th>
          <th>Notes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Day"><strong>Monday</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Exercise">Listen to one song and name each instrument you can hear</td>
          <td data-label="Notes">Write the list</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Day"><strong>Wednesday</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Exercise">Compare two versions of the same clip and say which is clearer</td>
          <td data-label="Notes">Give a reason</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Day"><strong>Friday</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Exercise">Record your own voice and note what you would change</td>
          <td data-label="Notes">Redo once</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Day"><strong>Weekend</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Exercise">Listen at low volume in a quiet room</td>
          <td data-label="Notes">Protect your hearing</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Keep volume moderate. Tired or damaged ears make poor decisions.
  </p>

